= Executive Summary
The scope of work for this project was to conduct an Arc Flash Hazard Analysis and provide an assessment of the current condition of the existing power distribution system at #sitename in #sitelocation. The study encompasses power distribution system from the incoming utilities down to the individual source panels.

In order to properly accomplish the Arc Flash Hazard Analysis, five steps were completed. First, the existing system information was accumulated from the owner or client supplied drawings and field investigation. Second, the system was entered into an electronic arc flash calculating computer program (SKM Systems Analysis Software) in a one line diagram format. The data entered included transformers, protective devices and cable sizes and lengths. Third, an electrical short circuit study was completed to establish the available fault current at each point of application or bus. Fourth, a protective device coordination was completed to coordinate the protective devices in the facility -- the representative Time Current Coordination curves are included in this documentation. Finally the arc flash hazard study was completed to evaluate the arc hazard levels for personnel that may be operating and/or working on the electrical power distribution system while it is energized. This report includes the short circuit, protective device coordination and arc flash studies.

== System Configuration
The electrical system at the #sitename in #sitelocation facility had not previously been modeled using the SKM software system. #preparedby used data gathered from single line drawings of the facility, first hand data gathering on-site, data gathered by an outside source and or 1st person interviews with maintenance staff. This model is considered a “snap shot” of the facility distribution system and does not reflect any future modifications of the system after the issue date of this report. As per NFPA-70E 130.5, it is recommended the study be updated every time a significant modification is made to the facility and reviewed periodically, not to exceed 5 years, to account for any system changes that may impact the design of the facilities protective systems.

The system is a single-phase 120/240V system. The SKM software edition #company used only models in three-phase components and calculations. This modeled system is not a perfect replication of the existing system but provides conservative short circuit & arc flash hazard values for the system. That is to say, the actual system may have lower short circuit or arc flash values, but not greater than those provided as a result of this study.

== Short Circuit Findings Summary
The system equipment is properly rated to withstand the available short circuit fault currents. No components were identified as being over-dutied and requiring replacement.

== Protective Device Coordination Findings Summary
The system contains almost no adjustable protective devices. The Generator breaker has an adjustable instantaneous setting which was set properly. TCCs have been provided to show the non-selective coordination of the fixed protective components in the system. Note that as of the completion of this study no devices were identified that require replacement. If a further degree of coordination is necessary or desired, additional equipment or equipment replacement would be required.

== Arc Flash Hazard Findings Summary
#ie-table()

Notes:
- Category 0 and Category 3 arc flash hazard levels are generally considered manageable and no corrective measures are typical taken for these hazard levels.
- Category 4 hazard levels are manageable but with significantly more PPE than Category 0 and 3 hazards. To reduce the Category 4 hazards in this electrical system additional equipment would be required.
- Areas identified as DANGEROUS – NO SAFE PPE EXISTS should not be worked on live. To reduce the DANGER hazards in this electrical system additional equipment would be required.
- Whenever possible, de-energize equipment before working on or around electrical parts. This should be the highest priority. It should be noted that PPE is required to confirm that the electrical parts are de-energized.

